qb('metrics', [
{n:21, q:`How would you measure the success of Google Photos?`,
tests:`Whether you start from the product's purpose, cover the user journey, and commit to one metric that reflects real value.`,
answer:`<p>I'd start with why Google Photos exists. For users, it does two jobs: it keeps your memories safe without you thinking about it, and it helps you relive and share them. For Google, it drives storage subscriptions and keeps people in the ecosystem. So success means people trust it with their photos and actually come back to enjoy them.</p>
<p><strong>The journey.</strong> Let me walk through it quickly. Getting started: someone installs Photos and turns on backup. Getting value: backup runs, and they find or share a photo. Coming back: they open it to look at memories, search, or share. Paying: they run out of free storage and subscribe.</p>
<p><strong>Metrics at each step.</strong> For getting started, the share of new users who turn on backup in their first week. For getting value, the share of photos backed up within a day of being taken. For coming back, weekly active users who do something meaningful, like viewing a memory, searching, or sharing, not just letting backup run silently. For paying, conversion to a storage plan and retention of paid users.</p>
<p><strong>The one I'd pick.</strong> Weekly users who engage with their photos, meaning they search, view memories, edit, or share. Here's why: backup alone makes Photos a utility, and utilities are easy to replace with whatever storage comes with your phone. People who regularly relive and share their photos get value that's hard to replace, and they're the ones who end up paying for storage.</p>
<p><strong>Guardrails.</strong> Backup reliability, because if backup fails, trust is gone and nothing else matters. And complaints about memories being unwelcome, like painful reminders. Engagement from memories is good, but not if it hurts people.</p>
<p>So in summary: the north star is weekly engaged users, backed by backup reliability as a must-not-break guardrail.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Purpose for users and for the business.</li><li>Walk the user journey.</li><li>Metrics at each stage.</li><li>Pick one north star, with a reason.</li><li>Guardrails.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Distinguishing passive backup from active engagement.</li><li>Linking the north star to the business model (storage).</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Choosing total photos stored or monthly active users, which don't capture value.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`Why not paid subscribers as the north star?`,`It's a result, not a driver. It lags, and pushing it directly could lead to aggressive paywalls. Engaged users are the leading indicator that drives subscriptions over time.`],[`How would you define "engage"?`,`I'd pick the actions most correlated with long-term retention, based on data. Likely viewing memories, search, and sharing.`]]},

{n:22, q:`What's the single most important metric for Google Maps?`,
tests:`Committing to one metric and defending it against alternatives.`,
answer:`<p>I'll start with what Maps promises: helping people get where they're going and discover places. The metric should reflect whether we delivered on that.</p>
<p><strong>The candidates.</strong> I'd compare three.</p>
<p>Monthly active users: broad and easy to track, but it tells me people opened the app, not that they got value. It also includes a lot of brief, low-intent visits.</p>
<p>Navigation trips started: a better signal of reliance, because starting navigation means someone trusted Maps to get them somewhere. But a started trip that ends in a wrong turn still counts.</p>
<p>Successful trips: trips where the person arrived, the estimated arrival time was accurate, and there was no major reroute or complaint. This measures whether the core promise was kept.</p>
<p><strong>My pick.</strong> Weekly successful trips per user. Three reasons. It reflects real value: someone got where they needed to go. It encourages the right work: better routing, better ETAs, better traffic data. And it's a habit signal: people who have good trips come back for the next one.</p>
<p><strong>The limitation</strong> is that it doesn't capture discovery, like finding a restaurant, which matters for Maps' local business and ad revenue. I'd track that as a secondary metric, for example place searches that lead to an action like directions or a call.</p>
<p><strong>Guardrails.</strong> ETA accuracy and safety reports, so we don't grow trips by, say, routing people through unsafe shortcuts to save a minute.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>State the product's promise.</li><li>Compare three candidates with pros and cons.</li><li>Commit to one with reasons.</li><li>Name the limitation and a secondary metric.</li><li>Guardrails.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Defining "successful" precisely.</li><li>Honestly naming what the metric misses.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Refusing to pick one ("it depends on the team").</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you measure that someone "arrived"?`,`When navigation ends near the destination and the phone stays there, rather than the person cancelling far away.`],[`Would the metric differ in a new market?`,`Early on, I might focus on reach, like weekly users and map coverage quality, before focusing on trip success.`]]},

{n:23, q:`Gmail daily active users dropped 5% week over week. Diagnose.`,
tests:`A structured investigation that rules out causes in a sensible order instead of guessing.`,
answer:`<p>I'd approach this in three stages: first, is the drop real? Then, where exactly is it? And then, why?</p>
<p><strong>Is it real?</strong> Before anything else, I'd check whether we changed how we measure. Was there a logging change, a data pipeline delay, or a dashboard definition change this week? I'd also check the calendar. A five percent drop week over week is normal if last week had a holiday in some countries, or this week does. I'd compare against the same week last year.</p>
<p><strong>Where is it?</strong> If it's real, I'd segment to find where it's concentrated. By platform: Android, iOS, web. By country. By app version. By user type: consumer versus Workspace, new versus existing users. By entry point: app open, notification, web. If the drop is spread evenly, it's more likely something external or a measurement issue. If it's concentrated, it points to a cause.</p>
<p><strong>Why?</strong> Then I'd match the pattern to possible causes.</p>
<ul><li>If it's one platform or app version: a release bug, like a crash on startup or broken notifications. I'd check crash rates and the release log.</li><li>If it's one country: a local outage, a network issue, or regulation.</li><li>If it's a drop in notification-driven opens: a change to notifications, or an operating system change affecting delivery.</li><li>If it's only new users: an issue with sign-up or a drop in Android device activations.</li><li>External: a competitor launch, or an event pulling attention.</li></ul>
<p><strong>What I'd do next.</strong> Once I find the likely cause, I'd confirm it. For example, if a new Android release broke notifications, I'd look at whether users on the old version are flat and users on the new version dropped. Then I'd fix or roll back, and set up an alert so we catch this faster next time.</p>
<p>I'd also communicate early: a short note to stakeholders saying what we know, what we're checking, and when we'll update.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Is it real? (measurement, seasonality)</li><li>Where is it? (segments)</li><li>Why? (map patterns to causes)</li><li>Confirm, fix, prevent, communicate.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Checking measurement and seasonality first, which most candidates skip.</li><li>Linking each segment pattern to a likely cause.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Listing random causes without a method for narrowing down.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`You find it's all on iOS. What next?`,`Check iOS app versions and iOS system version. If it lines up with a new iOS release, look at notification delivery and background refresh, which operating system updates often affect.`],[`How would you prevent this in the future?`,`Automated anomaly alerts on daily active users by segment, and release monitoring that compares new versions to old ones on key metrics.`]]},

{n:24, q:`Reported conversions for EU advertisers dropped 10% overnight. What do you do?`,
tests:`Diagnosis in the ads measurement domain, with awareness of consent, regulation, and customer impact.`,
answer:`<p>Overnight and limited to the EU makes me think of a few specific causes, but I'd go in order: triage, is it real, where, why, and then communicate.</p>
<p><strong>Triage.</strong> First, how many advertisers are affected, and is it still getting worse? If it's large and ongoing, I'd start an incident process right away, because advertisers' automated bidding uses these conversions, and a data drop can quickly reduce their bids and spend.</p>
<p><strong>Is it real, or reporting?</strong> I'd check whether this is a delay in processing, since conversions often arrive late, and a pipeline issue can look like a drop. And whether any reporting change shipped.</p>
<p><strong>Where?</strong> Segment it. Specific EU countries or all? Web conversions or app? Conversions from the tag, or uploaded from advertisers' systems? A specific browser? Advertisers using a particular consent tool?</p>
<p><strong>Why?</strong> The EU-specific causes I'd look at first:</p>
<ul><li>Consent: a change in a consent management platform, or in how consent signals are passed, could mean tags stop firing or we stop modeling for unconsented users.</li><li>A browser update, since browsers regularly change how cookies and tracking work.</li><li>A change on our side, like a new release of the tag or of the conversion modeling.</li><li>Regulation or a legal requirement that took effect.</li></ul>
<p>If it's concentrated in advertisers using one consent tool, for example, that's a strong signal.</p>
<p><strong>Communicate.</strong> I'd let account teams and affected advertisers know quickly: what we see, that we're investigating, and whether their bidding might be affected. If our system caused it, I'd consider protective steps, like telling bidding systems to discount the missing data, so advertisers don't see budget swings.</p>
<p><strong>After.</strong> Fix it, then do a review: why didn't we catch it faster, and what monitoring would have caught it? Tracking conversion volumes by region and consent status would be the obvious one.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Triage: scale and urgency.</li><li>Real versus reporting.</li><li>Segment to locate.</li><li>Domain-specific causes (consent, browsers, releases).</li><li>Communicate and protect customers.</li><li>Post-incident review.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Understanding that conversion drops feed into bidding, which raises urgency.</li><li>EU-specific hypotheses show domain knowledge.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Treating this like a generic metric drop with no customer urgency.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you tell a real drop in sales from a measurement problem?`,`Compare with advertisers' own backend data, like their order systems, for a sample of advertisers. If their sales are flat but our conversions dropped, it's measurement.`],[`What's conversion modeling?`,`When some users don't consent to tracking, we estimate the conversions we can't observe, using aggregated patterns from users who did consent. A bug there would show up as a drop exactly like this.`]]},

{n:25, q:`In an A/B test, click-through rate and ad revenue rose, but user satisfaction fell. Do you ship?`,
tests:`Judgment about short-term revenue versus long-term user trust.`,
answer:`<p>My default answer is no, not as is. But I'd want to understand the size and nature of the trade-off before deciding.</p>
<p><strong>Why the default is no.</strong> For an ads business, user satisfaction is a leading indicator. If people find the experience worse, they gradually use the product less, and future revenue suffers. Short tests often catch the revenue gain but miss the slow decline in usage that comes later.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd look at.</strong></p>
<ul><li>How big is each effect, and are both statistically solid? A two percent revenue gain with a small satisfaction dip is different from a small gain with a big dip.</li><li>What's driving the satisfaction drop? If clicks rose because ads look more like organic results, that's a red flag: people may be clicking by mistake. I'd check the share of quick bounces back from the ad.</li><li>Did long-term signals move? For example, searches per user or return rate during the test.</li><li>Who's affected? Is it a specific segment, like mobile users or certain query types?</li></ul>
<p><strong>What I'd do.</strong> If the click increase comes from accidental clicks, I wouldn't ship, because that's also bad for advertisers who pay for worthless clicks. If it's a genuine trade-off, I'd look for a version that keeps most of the revenue with less harm, like showing the new format only on high-intent commercial queries. And I'd run a longer holdout to measure long-term effects before any full launch.</p>
<p><strong>The principle</strong> I'd state is that we set guardrails before the test, and user satisfaction is one of them. If we cross a guardrail, the default is not to ship, and overriding that should require a clear, explicit decision by leadership, not a quiet trade.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>State a default position.</li><li>Explain why (leading indicator).</li><li>What you'd investigate.</li><li>Decision rules for each case.</li><li>The principle of pre-set guardrails.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Checking for accidental clicks shows ads domain knowledge.</li><li>Seeking a version that keeps gains with less harm.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>"Ship it, revenue is up," or "never ship" with no analysis.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`Leadership wants the revenue. What do you say?`,`I'd quantify the risk: show the long-term holdout results, or estimate the lost usage. Then present options, including a narrower launch, and let them decide with full information.`],[`How would you measure long-term effects?`,`A long-running holdout group that doesn't get the change, compared over several months on usage and revenue.`]]},

{n:26, q:`An experiment shows +2% conversions with p = 0.09. Ship?`,
tests:`Understanding of statistics in practice, and making decisions under uncertainty based on risk.`,
answer:`<p>It depends, and I'll explain what it depends on.</p>
<p><strong>What the result means.</strong> A p-value of 0.09 means that if the change had no real effect, we'd still see a result at least this large about nine percent of the time. So it's suggestive, but weaker evidence than our usual five percent threshold. It doesn't mean there's no effect, and it doesn't mean there's a ninety-one percent chance it works.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd check.</strong></p>
<ul><li>Did the test run for its planned length and sample size? If we're looking early, this number could still change a lot.</li><li>What's the confidence interval? If it's something like minus half a percent to plus four and a half percent, the worst case is a small loss, and most of the range is positive.</li><li>Guardrail metrics: did anything else get worse?</li><li>Is the result consistent across segments and across the weeks of the test? A result that holds steadily is more believable than one driven by one day.</li></ul>
<p><strong>How I'd decide.</strong> Mostly by the cost of being wrong. If the change is cheap, easily reversible, and has no downside in the guardrails, like a button color, I'd lean toward shipping or extending the test. The expected value is positive and we can undo it. If the change is expensive to maintain, hard to reverse, or has any hint of harm, I'd extend the test to reach the planned sample, rather than lowering our standard because we like the result.</p>
<p>What I wouldn't do is stop the test now because the number looks good, since that inflates false positives over time.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Explain the p-value correctly.</li><li>Checks: sample, interval, guardrails, consistency.</li><li>Decide based on cost of being wrong.</li><li>Warn against peeking.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>A correct, plain explanation of the p-value.</li><li>Tying the decision to reversibility.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Saying "it's not significant, so no effect."</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`What's the risk of peeking at results early?`,`If you check repeatedly and stop when it looks good, you'll call many random ups as wins. Either pre-commit to a sample, or use methods designed for continuous monitoring.`],[`How would you make future tests more conclusive?`,`Estimate the needed sample size before starting based on the smallest effect worth detecting, and use variance reduction techniques, like adjusting for users' pre-test behavior.`]]},

{n:27, q:`What metrics would you use for a new CRM connector in a data-management product?`,
tests:`Metrics for a B2B integration, covering adoption, reliability, and the value it ultimately creates.`,
answer:`<p>A CRM connector moves a business's customer data, like leads and sales, into our product, where it's used for measurement and targeting. Its value isn't the connection itself. It's what the data enables: better conversion measurement and better ad performance. So my metrics follow that chain.</p>
<p><strong>Adoption.</strong> How many eligible customers connect it? I'd measure the share of eligible accounts that start setup, and the share that finish. The gap between those two tells me where setup is hard.</p>
<p><strong>Health.</strong> Once connected, is data flowing reliably? The share of connections that synced successfully in the last day, data freshness, meaning time from an event in the CRM to it arriving in our system, and error rates, like mapping failures.</p>
<p><strong>Quality.</strong> Is the data useful? For example, the match rate between CRM records and users, and the share of records with the fields we need.</p>
<p><strong>Value.</strong> Is it improving outcomes? For advertisers, conversions attributed to CRM data, like offline sales, and ideally a performance lift, for example better cost per acquisition after connecting, compared with similar accounts that haven't connected.</p>
<p><strong>The north star</strong> I'd choose is the number of accounts with healthy, active CRM data flowing into campaigns, meaning connected, syncing in the last week, and used by at least one campaign or conversion action. That combines adoption, health, and use in one number, and it can't be inflated by connections that were set up and then abandoned.</p>
<p><strong>Guardrails.</strong> Data errors and privacy issues, like fields that shouldn't be uploaded, and support tickets per connection.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Explain what value the connector creates.</li><li>Metrics along the chain: adoption, health, quality, value.</li><li>One north star that combines them.</li><li>Guardrails.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>A north star that excludes abandoned connections.</li><li>Recognizing data freshness as a metric.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Stopping at "number of connections."</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`Setup completion is low. What would you do?`,`Look at where people drop, often authentication or field mapping. Then simplify: auto-map common fields and add clear error messages.`],[`How would you prove the connector improves performance?`,`An experiment where some accounts get recommended to connect earlier, or a comparison with matched accounts that didn't connect, measuring change in conversion volume and cost.`]]},

{n:28, q:`What guardrail metrics would you set for launching a new ad format?`,
tests:`Understanding the three sides of the ads ecosystem and what can go wrong for each.`,
answer:`<p>Any new ad format has three groups it can affect: users, advertisers, and publishers or the platform. The primary metric is usually revenue or advertiser performance. Guardrails make sure we don't get that by hurting someone.</p>
<p><strong>For users.</strong> The main risk is a worse experience. I'd watch: user satisfaction on pages with the format, measured by surveys; ad hide and report rates; accidental click rate, which we can estimate from very quick bounces; and longer-term usage, like searches or sessions per user. And page speed, since new formats can slow loading.</p>
<p><strong>For advertisers.</strong> The risk is paying for low-quality engagement. I'd watch conversion rate and cost per conversion for clicks from the new format, compared with existing formats. If clicks rise but conversions don't, the format is generating low-value clicks.</p>
<p><strong>For the platform and publishers.</strong> Cannibalization: does the new format simply take revenue from existing ones? So total revenue per page, not just revenue from the new format. And policy issues, like new ways to create misleading ads.</p>
<p><strong>How I'd use them.</strong> I'd set thresholds before launch, for example "user satisfaction can't drop more than half a point," and agree with leadership that crossing a threshold means we don't ship without explicit review. I'd also keep a long-term holdout, because some harms, like people slowly using the product less, show up only after weeks.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Name the three groups.</li><li>Guardrails for each.</li><li>How to use guardrails: thresholds set before launch, plus a long-term holdout.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Covering all sides of the marketplace.</li><li>Cannibalization, which many candidates miss.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Listing metrics without saying how they'd be used.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`What's the most important guardrail?`,`Long-term user engagement, because it protects everything else. But it's slow, so I'd rely on satisfaction and accidental clicks as early proxies.`],[`How would you set the threshold?`,`Based on the normal week-to-week variation of the metric and a view on what change would matter, agreed with stakeholders before the test.`]]},

{n:29, q:`What metrics would you use to evaluate YouTube Shorts?`,
tests:`Metrics for a two-sided product, and understanding a product's role within a larger platform.`,
answer:`<p>Shorts plays a specific role for YouTube: competing with other short-video apps for attention, bringing in new and younger viewers, and giving creators a fast way to grow. So I'd measure it on three fronts: viewers, creators, and its effect on YouTube overall.</p>
<p><strong>Viewers.</strong> Daily active viewers of Shorts, and watch time per viewer. But I'd go beyond raw time: I'd look at the share of Shorts watched to completion, and the rate of likes, shares, and follows. Those tell me the content is good, not just that people are scrolling.</p>
<p><strong>Creators.</strong> Number of active creators posting weekly, the share of new creators who keep posting, and the distribution of views: are they spread across many creators, or concentrated at the top? A healthy platform lets new creators break through.</p>
<p><strong>YouTube overall.</strong> This is the key question: does Shorts add to YouTube, or just move time from long-form videos? I'd look at total YouTube watch time for users who adopt Shorts compared with similar users who don't. And whether Shorts viewers go on to watch the same creators' long-form videos.</p>
<p><strong>Business.</strong> Ad revenue per thousand views, and how that trends, since short-form monetization is harder.</p>
<p><strong>North star.</strong> I'd choose daily active Shorts viewers who have a satisfying session, measured through completion and engagement, because it captures both reach and quality. With a guardrail on total YouTube watch time per user, so we don't grow Shorts by cannibalizing long-form.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Define the product's role in the platform.</li><li>Metrics for viewers, creators, the platform, the business.</li><li>A north star plus a platform-level guardrail.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Cannibalization and spillover to long-form.</li><li>Creator distribution as a health metric.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Only measuring views.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`Shorts watch time is up but long-form is down. Is that bad?`,`Only if total YouTube value is down. I'd look at total watch time, revenue, and satisfaction. If users prefer Shorts and total value is stable, it's a shift, not a loss.`],[`How would you measure content quality?`,`Completion rate, engagement, and survey-based satisfaction, since watch time alone can reward content that's compelling but unsatisfying.`]]},

{n:30, q:`What's the most important metric for Google Drive?`,
tests:`Choosing a metric that reflects real value for a storage and collaboration product.`,
answer:`<p>Drive does two things: it stores files safely, and it lets people work on them together. The storage part drives paid plans. The collaboration part, through Docs, Sheets, and Slides, is what makes it hard to leave. So the metric should reflect active use, not just storage.</p>
<p><strong>Candidates.</strong> Total storage used: easy, but it rewards people dumping files they never open. Monthly active users: broad, but includes people who opened one shared link. Weekly users who create, edit, or share a file: this reflects Drive being part of how someone works.</p>
<p><strong>My pick.</strong> Weekly active collaborators: users who create, edit, or share a file with at least one other person in a week. Collaboration is what makes Drive sticky. When your team's files live there, switching costs are high, and it pulls new users in through shared links. It also matters for Workspace, where businesses pay per seat because their teams collaborate there.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting metrics.</strong> For the consumer business, conversion to paid storage plans. For reliability, sync success and time to open files.</p>
<p><strong>Guardrails.</strong> Security incidents, like files shared publicly by mistake. Growing sharing is good, but not if people accidentally expose sensitive files.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>The product's two jobs.</li><li>Compare candidates.</li><li>Pick one with a reason tied to stickiness and business.</li><li>Supporting metrics and guardrails.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Recognizing collaboration as the moat.</li><li>A security guardrail relevant to sharing.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Storage volume as the north star.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`Would the metric differ for business customers?`,`For Workspace, I'd track active collaborators per paid seat, since unused seats predict churn at renewal.`],[`How would you grow the metric?`,`Make sharing easier and more obvious, for example suggesting sharing a doc with the people in a related calendar event.`]]},

{n:31, q:`Search query volume dropped 3% week over week. Diagnose.`,
tests:`Structured diagnosis for a critical, very large product, with good instincts about normal variation.`,
answer:`<p>For Search, three percent is significant but not unusual week to week, so I'd first figure out whether it's real and abnormal, then locate it, then explain it.</p>
<p><strong>Is it real and abnormal?</strong> I'd compare against the same week last year. Holidays, school breaks, and major events all move search volume. A big news event the previous week would inflate last week's number and make this week look like a drop. I'd also rule out logging or pipeline changes, and changes to how we count, for example filtering bot traffic.</p>
<p><strong>Where is it?</strong> Segment by device, country, access point, like the Google app, Chrome, other browsers, and partner integrations, and by query type, like news, shopping, and navigational.</p>
<p><strong>Why?</strong> Match to causes:</p>
<ul><li>A drop in one access point, like searches from a specific browser: possibly a default search setting changed, or a partner integration broke.</li><li>A drop in one category, like shopping: seasonality, or users moving to another platform for that need.</li><li>A drop in a specific country: an outage, local regulation, or network issues.</li><li>A drop in questions that an AI answer now satisfies: people may be finding answers without needing to search again. That's not necessarily bad, if satisfaction is up.</li><li>A drop in navigational queries, like "facebook login": could be a browser change that sends people directly.</li></ul>
<p><strong>Next steps.</strong> Confirm with a targeted look at the suspected segment. If there's a bug, fix it. If it's a behavior shift, like people using AI assistants for certain questions, that's a strategic signal worth raising with leadership, with data on which query types are affected.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Is it real and abnormal?</li><li>Segment.</li><li>Match patterns to causes.</li><li>Confirm, fix, or escalate strategically.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Distinguishing bugs from real behavior shifts.</li><li>Recognizing that fewer searches can mean better answers.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Assuming every drop is a problem to fix.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`What if the drop is due to AI answers satisfying people faster?`,`Then query volume isn't the right measure of success. I'd shift attention to satisfaction and task completion, and monitor ad revenue per task.`],[`How would you catch this faster next time?`,`Automated anomaly detection by segment, adjusted for seasonality, with alerts to the owning teams.`]]},

{n:32, q:`What's the north star metric for Google Tag Manager?`,
tests:`Understanding an infrastructure product whose value appears elsewhere, in measurement and ad performance.`,
answer:`<p>Tag Manager is infrastructure. It lets businesses add and manage measurement tags on their website without editing code each time. Its value isn't in Tag Manager itself. It's in accurate data flowing to tools like Google Ads and Analytics. So the north star should reflect that value, not how often people use the interface.</p>
<p><strong>Candidates.</strong> Number of containers created: easy but inflated by abandoned setups. Monthly active users of the interface: this actually goes down when the product works well, because nobody needs to touch a working setup. Tags firing correctly: closer to value.</p>
<p><strong>My pick.</strong> Active containers delivering healthy data to at least one destination, meaning containers installed on a live site, with tags firing successfully, sending events that are received by, for example, a conversion action or an analytics property in the last week.</p>
<p>Why? It reflects the whole chain: installed, working, and useful. It encourages the right work, like making setup easier and diagnosing broken tags. And it connects to Google's business: healthy measurement data improves ad performance, which drives advertiser spend.</p>
<p><strong>Supporting metrics.</strong> Time from creating a container to first healthy data, the share of containers with errors, and publishing frequency, as a measure of ongoing engagement.</p>
<p><strong>Guardrails.</strong> Page speed impact, since tags can slow websites, and privacy compliance, like tags respecting consent settings.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Explain it's infrastructure and where value appears.</li><li>Candidates, including why usage can mislead.</li><li>A north star that captures the chain.</li><li>Supporting metrics, guardrails.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Noting that interface usage can fall when things work well.</li><li>Connecting to Google's ads business.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Choosing interface active users.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you count "healthy data"?`,`Tags that fired and whose events were received and accepted by the destination, without errors, in the last seven days.`],[`What would you do to grow it?`,`Focus on the biggest leaks: containers created but never installed, and installed but broken. Guided setup and automated health checks.`]]},

{n:33, q:`Container publishes in a tag manager dropped 8% week over week. Diagnose.`,
tests:`Diagnosis for a B2B tool, including judging whether a drop is actually bad.`,
answer:`<p>Before diving in, I'd ask whether this is even a problem. Publishing is when someone pushes changes to their tags live. Fewer publishes could mean something is broken, or it could mean people's setups are stable and they don't need to make changes. So I'll figure out whether it's real, where it is, and whether it's bad.</p>
<p><strong>Is it real?</strong> Check for logging or pipeline changes, and seasonality. B2B tools often dip in holiday weeks. Also, marketing activity is seasonal: publishing tends to spike before big campaigns, like the shopping season, and drop after.</p>
<p><strong>Where is it?</strong> Segment by account size: agencies and large companies versus small businesses. By region. By how publishing happens: through the interface or through the API, since large customers often automate it. By new versus existing containers.</p>
<p><strong>Possible causes.</strong></p>
<ul><li>A bug in the publish flow: I'd check error rates on publish attempts. If attempts are stable but successes dropped, that's a bug.</li><li>An API change affecting automated publishing: if the drop is concentrated in API publishes.</li><li>A drop in new containers: if new users are down, they publish a lot during setup, so publishes would fall. That points upstream to acquisition.</li><li>A product change that reduced the need to publish, like a new feature allowing changes without publishing.</li><li>Normal: fewer campaigns after a busy season.</li></ul>
<p><strong>What I'd do.</strong> If it's a bug, fix it urgently, because a failed publish means customers can't update their tracking. If it's seasonal, note it and move on. And I'd consider whether publishes is the right metric to monitor, because healthy data flowing matters more than the number of changes.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Question whether the drop is bad.</li><li>Real versus seasonal.</li><li>Segment.</li><li>Causes, including attempts versus successes.</li><li>Act and reconsider the metric.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Attempts versus successes is a clean diagnostic.</li><li>Questioning the metric itself.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Assuming fewer publishes is always bad.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`What would you monitor instead?`,`Share of containers with healthy data flow, and publish failure rate.`],[`You find publish errors spiked. What now?`,`Check recent releases, roll back if needed, identify affected customers, and notify them, since some may have tried to fix tracking and failed.`]]},

{n:34, q:`An A/B test shows engagement up for new users but down for power users. What do you decide?`,
tests:`Handling conflicting segment results, and connecting the decision to product strategy.`,
answer:`<p>I'd make this decision based on three things: the size of each effect, how much each group matters to the product's goals, and whether we can avoid the trade-off entirely.</p>
<p><strong>First, understand the effect.</strong> How big is the lift for new users and the drop for power users? Is it statistically solid for both? And why: what is it about the change that helps new users and hurts power users? Often it's a simplification: something that makes the product easier to learn but removes shortcuts or density that experts rely on.</p>
<p><strong>Second, weigh the groups.</strong> Power users are often a small share of users but a large share of activity, revenue, and content. They also influence others, for example by creating content or recommending the product. New users are the future of the product. So I'd look at the net effect on the metric that matters, like total engagement or revenue, and at the long-term picture: will new users become power users with this change, or will this change stop them from getting there?</p>
<p><strong>Third, avoid the trade-off.</strong> Usually the best answer is not choosing one group. We can often give new users the simpler experience and let power users keep their workflow. For example, show the new design to people in their first few weeks, or let experienced users switch back to the advanced view.</p>
<p><strong>My decision.</strong> I wouldn't ship it to everyone. I'd ship it to new users, keep the current experience for power users, and follow the new users over time to see whether they become as engaged as today's power users. If maintaining two versions is too costly long-term, I'd iterate on a version that works for both.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Understand the size and cause.</li><li>Weigh the groups by value and future impact.</li><li>Look for a way to avoid the trade-off.</li><li>Decide and plan to follow up.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Explaining why the split happens (simplification).</li><li>Targeting the change instead of choosing a group.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Averaging the effects and moving on.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`What if engineering can't support two versions?`,`Then I'd choose based on the net effect and strategy, and work to recover what power users lost, for example keyboard shortcuts or a denser view option.`],[`How would you define power users?`,`Top ten percent by activity over the last month, or users with specific advanced behaviors, depending on the product.`]]},

{n:35, q:`Your A/B test has a sample ratio mismatch (52/48 when 50/50 was planned). What do you do?`,
tests:`Rigor about experiment validity and knowing when not to trust results.`,
answer:`<p>I wouldn't trust any of the results until I've found the cause. A sample ratio mismatch means the groups aren't what we designed, so something is systematically different between them, and that can bias every metric.</p>
<p><strong>Is it real?</strong> With a large sample, 52 to 48 is almost certainly not random. I'd confirm with a quick statistical test, but with, say, a hundred thousand users per group, this is far beyond chance.</p>
<p><strong>Common causes.</strong></p>
<ul><li>Assignment bugs: the code that splits users isn't randomizing correctly, or a group is being assigned based on some attribute.</li><li>Logging differences: one variant loses more events, for example it crashes more or loads slower, so users are assigned but never recorded. This is the most common cause, and it's the dangerous one, because the users who disappear are probably the ones having a bad experience.</li><li>Redirects or eligibility: one variant sends users through an extra step, and some drop off before being counted.</li><li>Bots or filtering: bot traffic removed differently from each group.</li></ul>
<p><strong>How I'd find it.</strong> Check whether the mismatch is consistent across days, browsers, countries, and platforms. If it's only on one browser or app version, that points to a logging or crash issue in that environment.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd do.</strong> Pause decisions on this test. Fix the cause, then restart the test with clean data. I wouldn't try to reweight the groups, because I don't know what's different about the missing users.</p>
<p>I'd also check whether the mismatch itself tells us something. If variant B has fewer users recorded because it crashes more, that's a very important finding about variant B.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Don't trust results; explain why.</li><li>Confirm it's real.</li><li>Common causes.</li><li>Segment to find it.</li><li>Fix and rerun; note what the mismatch reveals.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Knowing that logging loss is the most common cause, and why it biases results.</li><li>Refusing to reweight.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>"It's close to 50/50, so it's fine."</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you prevent this in future?`,`Automatic sample ratio checks on every experiment, with alerts and results hidden until they pass.`],[`What if the business needs a decision this week?`,`I'd explain that the results may be biased in an unknown direction. If there's a clear cause that I can isolate, like one broken browser, I might analyze the unaffected segment with caveats.`]]},

{n:36, q:`How would you measure the impact of ads on brand awareness when no purchase happens?`,
tests:`Knowledge of brand measurement and thinking about causal measurement without a conversion event.`,
answer:`<p>Brand campaigns aim to change what people think and remember, not to get a click or a sale right away. So I'd measure changes in awareness and attitudes, and ideally connect them to later behavior.</p>
<p><strong>The main method: brand lift studies.</strong> Split the target audience randomly: one group sees the ads, and a control group doesn't. Then survey both groups with short questions, like "Which of these brands have you heard of?" or "How likely are you to consider this brand?" The difference between the groups is the lift caused by the ads. Because the groups are randomized, it measures causation, not just correlation.</p>
<p><strong>Behavioral signals.</strong> Surveys only reach a sample, so I'd also look at behavior. Search lift: do people who saw the ads search for the brand more than the control group? Site visits: do more of them visit the brand's website directly? These are signs of increased interest.</p>
<p><strong>Longer term.</strong> For the brand's business, the real question is whether awareness eventually leads to sales. That's harder, because the time between seeing an ad and buying can be months. Marketing mix modeling, which uses aggregated data on spend and sales over time, can estimate the long-term contribution of brand advertising.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd recommend.</strong> For a single campaign: a brand lift study with search lift as a supporting metric. For the advertiser's overall strategy: marketing mix modeling that includes brand spend. And I'd advise setting clear goals before the campaign, like "increase awareness among eighteen- to thirty-four-year-olds by five points," so the results lead to a decision.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Explain what brand campaigns aim to change.</li><li>Primary method: randomized survey-based lift.</li><li>Behavioral signals.</li><li>Long-term modeling.</li><li>Recommendation with goal-setting.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Emphasizing randomization for causality.</li><li>Layering methods by time horizon.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Only suggesting impressions or reach.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`What are the limits of survey-based lift?`,`Small samples for niche audiences, survey response bias, and it measures stated attitudes, not actual behavior.`],[`How would you explain the results to a CMO?`,`"Among people who saw the campaign, eight percent more remembered your brand than among similar people who didn't. And they searched for you twelve percent more."`]]},

{n:37, q:`An advertiser's match rate is 85%, yet attributed conversions barely rose after uploading first-party data. Why?`,
tests:`Deep domain understanding of first-party data and how it does or doesn't affect measurement.`,
answer:`<p>A high match rate only means we could link their customer records to users. It doesn't guarantee more attributed conversions. There are several reasons why the two can diverge, and I'd check them in order.</p>
<p><strong>What was uploaded, and how is it used?</strong> If they uploaded a customer list for targeting, that affects who sees ads, not how conversions are measured. Match rate is high, but it wouldn't add conversions to reports. If they intended to improve measurement, they need something like enhanced conversions or offline conversion imports, which attach first-party data to conversion events.</p>
<p><strong>Were those conversions already being counted?</strong> If the advertiser's tag was already working well, most conversions were already measured. The first-party data helps recover the ones that were missed, for example when cookies were unavailable. If only a small share was missing, the lift will be small. That's actually good news: their measurement was already solid.</p>
<p><strong>Timing.</strong> New data can take days to be processed, and conversion windows mean effects appear gradually. I'd check whether enough time has passed.</p>
<p><strong>Configuration issues.</strong> For example, the data was uploaded but not attached to the right conversion action. Or the hashed fields are formatted incorrectly for the conversion data, even though they matched fine for the list. Or duplicates are being removed correctly, so the number looks flat because previously double-counted conversions were fixed.</p>
<p><strong>What I'd do.</strong> Clarify the goal with the advertiser, check the setup of the conversion action, look at diagnostics, and then explain in plain terms: "Your match rate means your data is good. Here's why reported conversions didn't jump, and here's where the benefit shows up instead," for example in bidding performance.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Explain why match rate and attributed conversions differ.</li><li>Check how the data is used.</li><li>Baseline coverage.</li><li>Timing.</li><li>Configuration.</li><li>Communicate.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Separating targeting lists from measurement data.</li><li>Seeing small lift as a sign of good existing measurement.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Blaming the advertiser's data without investigation.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you make this less confusing in the product?`,`Show a clear status for each data upload: what it's being used for, and its expected effect, with a diagnostic that estimates recovered conversions.`],[`How would you estimate how many conversions were missing?`,`Compare conversions reported by the tag with the advertiser's backend order data over the same period.`]]},

{n:38, q:`How would you explain correlation versus causation in ad measurement to a CMO?`,
tests:`Clear communication of a technical concept to a senior, busy, business-focused audience.`,
answer:`<p>I'd keep it short, concrete, and tied to their budget. Something like this:</p>
<p>"Most ad reports tell you that people who saw your ad went on to buy. That's correlation. It's true, but it doesn't tell you whether the ad caused the purchase.</p>
<p>Here's an example. Imagine you show ads to people who searched for your brand name. Many of them buy. The report says your ads drove those sales. But these people were already looking for you. Many would have bought anyway, ad or no ad.</p>
<p>Causation asks a different question: how many sales would you lose if you turned the ads off? That's the number that matters for your budget.</p>
<p>The way to measure it is an experiment. We take a random group of people, or regions, and don't show them the ads. Then we compare their sales with the group that did see them. The difference is what the ads actually caused.</p>
<p>What this means for you: some campaigns look great in reports but add little in reality, and others look average but drive real growth. Running a few experiments on your biggest campaigns tells you where to put your next dollar."</p>
<p>Then I'd pause and ask whether they'd like to run a test on a specific campaign. The point of the explanation is to lead to a decision, not a lecture.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Define correlation in one line.</li><li>A relatable example (brand search).</li><li>Reframe causation as a budget question.</li><li>How to measure it simply.</li><li>What it means for their decisions, then a call to action.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>The brand-search example is instantly understandable.</li><li>Ending with a decision, not theory.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Statistical jargon or a long lecture.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`The CMO asks why they can't just trust the attribution report.`,`"It's useful for day-to-day optimization, like which ad or keyword to adjust. But for big budget decisions, like how much to spend on a channel, an experiment is the best evidence."`],[`What if they worry about losing sales during the test?`,`We keep the holdout small, for example five to ten percent, and short. The cost is modest compared with the value of knowing where the budget really works.`]]},

{n:101, q:`How would you set up a metrics framework for a brand-new AI feature with no historical data?`,
tests:`Designing measurement from first principles, including quality, which is often invisible in usage data.`,
answer:`<p>With no history, I can't compare to a baseline, so I'd build the framework from what the feature is supposed to do, and set up the baselines as we go. Let me use an example: an AI feature that drafts replies to customer emails.</p>
<p><strong>Start with the job.</strong> What does success look like for the user? A draft that saves them time, and that they're comfortable sending. So there are two sides: usefulness and quality.</p>
<p><strong>Layer one: adoption.</strong> How many eligible users try it, and how many use it again in their second week. The second number matters more.</p>
<p><strong>Layer two: usefulness.</strong> Acceptance rate, meaning drafts sent with little or no editing, and the amount of editing. If people rewrite most of every draft, it's not saving time. Time to send a reply, compared with users who don't have the feature.</p>
<p><strong>Layer three: quality and safety.</strong> This is what usage data alone won't show. I'd create an evaluation process: regularly sample outputs and have people rate them for accuracy, tone, and whether they'd be comfortable sending. Plus tracking of reported bad outputs.</p>
<p><strong>Layer four: business and cost.</strong> Whether it improves retention or customer satisfaction for the businesses using it, and the cost per draft, since AI features have real running costs.</p>
<p><strong>Getting baselines.</strong> I'd launch to a limited group with a holdout that doesn't get the feature, so we can compare outcomes like response time and customer satisfaction. And set early targets as hypotheses: "We expect forty percent of drafts sent with light edits," then adjust once we have data.</p>
<p><strong>North star.</strong> Weekly users who send at least one AI-assisted reply with minimal editing. It combines adoption, usefulness, and quality in one number.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Pick a concrete example.</li><li>Define the job and success.</li><li>Layered metrics: adoption, usefulness, quality, business and cost.</li><li>How to create baselines.</li><li>North star.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Explicit quality evaluation beyond usage.</li><li>Holdout for baselines, and targets framed as hypotheses.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Measuring only usage for an AI feature.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`How would you scale quality evaluation?`,`Use a rubric with human raters on a sample, then train or use an automated evaluator calibrated against those ratings, and keep checking it against humans.`],[`What if usage is high but quality ratings are low?`,`That's a warning sign. People may be accepting drafts they'd later regret. I'd prioritize quality before growing usage further.`]]},

{n:102, q:`A metric you rely on for decision-making is revealed to have a measurement bug that's been present for six months. What do you do?`,
tests:`Integrity, handling a mistake with transparency, and managing impact on past decisions.`,
answer:`<p>I'd handle this in four steps: understand the bug, assess which decisions it affected, communicate openly, and fix the process.</p>
<p><strong>Understand the bug.</strong> What exactly was wrong, and in which direction? Was it over- or under-counting, and by how much? Did it affect everyone equally, or only some segments? A bug that shifts a number uniformly by two percent is very different from one that distorts comparisons between groups.</p>
<p><strong>Assess the impact.</strong> I'd list the decisions made using this metric in the last six months, like launches, experiments, and goal-setting, and for each ask: would the decision have been different with correct data? For most, probably not. For some, the decision might flip. Where possible, I'd recompute the correct numbers for past data.</p>
<p><strong>Communicate.</strong> Quickly and openly. I'd tell my manager and the teams relying on the metric what happened, what we know, and which decisions might need revisiting. I wouldn't wait until I have every answer, and I wouldn't minimize it. Trust in data depends on being straight about mistakes. If the metric was reported externally, for example to customers, that needs to be handled carefully with the relevant teams.</p>
<p><strong>Fix and prevent.</strong> Fix the bug, backfill corrected data where possible, and ask how it went undetected for six months. Usually the answer is missing validation, like no comparison to an independent data source. I'd add checks: automated alerts when the metric moves unusually, and periodic reconciliation with another source.</p>
<p>And for decisions that turn out to have been wrong, I'd propose what to do, for example re-running an experiment, rather than just flagging it.</p>`,
notes:`<h4>Structure under the answer</h4><ol><li>Understand the bug and its direction.</li><li>Audit affected decisions.</li><li>Communicate early and openly.</li><li>Fix, backfill, prevent, and propose remedies.</li></ol><h4>What makes it strong</h4><ul><li>Asking whether the bug is uniform or distorts comparisons.</li><li>Proactively proposing fixes for wrong decisions.</li></ul><h4>Avoid</h4><ul><li>Hiding or downplaying the issue.</li></ul>`,
follow:[[`What if it was your team's bug?`,`Own it clearly, apologize, and focus the conversation on impact and the fix. Owning it quickly builds more trust than a perfect record.`],[`How would you decide which past decisions to revisit?`,`Prioritize by how much the correct data changes the conclusion and how costly the decision is to reverse.`]]}
]);
